    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Three Songs for Jerusalem, for soprano, Baroque flute, Baroque oboe, Baroque violin,\r\nviola da gamba, and harpsichord,was comissioned by the Jerusalem Foundation for the celebration of the 3000th \r\nanniversary of the City of Jerusalem. \r\nThe first song is from the 12th centruy poet and philosopher Yehuda Halevi's \r\ndescription of a traveller's first view of the Holy Land seen from the wings of an \r\neagle. The second song combines a biblical call to the daughters of Jerusalem to \r\nrejoise, with lines from contemporary poet Yehuda Amichai, who hears the calls to \r\nprayer in the city amidst the din of air raid sirens. The final song quotes Isaac \r\nManger's post-Holocaust  answer to Halevi's image by asking how we can kiss \r\nthe dust of the Holy Land when we, ourselves, are dust.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
